---
// The scroll box of one of the docs' own tables: Tanrei's Table (specs/table.spec.json) around the
// native `<table>` the calling component writes as its child. The caller keeps its table — its header cells, its row
// headers (`<th scope="row">`), its cells' `data-align` and `data-wrap="false"` — and this says how it is drawn.
// A column's `data-align` and `data-wrap="false"` are written on its header cell AND on each of its cells: the element
// gives the header's word to the column when it is defined, and a page is drawn before that, and without script
// (specs/table.spec.json, the web's note on a column). What a header cell says is therefore its whole column's:
// `data-wrap="false"` on the header of a column of running text puts that text on one line once the element is
// defined, so such a header does not say it.
// Markdown tables get the same wrapper at build time (lib/markdown.mjs). The stylesheet is loaded with the site's
// other Tanrei sheets (styles/prose.css), so a table is drawn in the first frame; the element adds the focus stop of
// a box that scrolls, the two "there is more" lines, and the table's name.
//
// Code in one of these tables is inline code in its plain form (`data-tanrei-code="plain"`: the code face, no fill): a
// column of property names, token names or requirement ids is a column of names, not of chips. The class itself is
// written by `MarkdownContent.astro`, for every `code` of a page.
interface Props {
  /** The table's accessible name (it has no caption): what the heading above it says. */
  label: string;
  density?: 'regular' | 'compact';
  /** The first column names its rows. */
  rowHeaders?: boolean;
  /** The first column stays in view while the others scroll under it: for a wide table whose first column names the row. */
  stickyFirstColumn?: boolean;
  /** The page orders this table's rows when a header asks (lib/sort-table.ts, loaded by the caller): the Table itself never sorts. */
  sorts?: boolean;
  class?: string;
  /** Custom properties the caller's own rules read on the box (the room a table holds before it is laid out). */
  style?: string;
}
const { label, density, rowHeaders = false, stickyFirstColumn = false, sorts = false, class: className, style } = Astro.props;
---

<tanrei-table class={className} style={style} data-tanrei-code="plain" label={label} density={density} row-headers={rowHeaders ? '' : undefined} sticky-first-column={stickyFirstColumn ? '' : undefined} data-docs-sorts={sorts ? '' : undefined}><slot /></tanrei-table>

<script>
  import '@tanrei/web/components/table';
</script>
